b587.6ab92b064fc57f017021e8a8b41155e9.png\"><noscript><img class=\"g-image g-image--lazy g-image--loaded\" src=\"/assets/static/tutorial-blog-project.42db587.6ab92b064fc57f017021e8a8b41155e9.png\" width=\"2560\" alt=\"Firebase-I\"></noscript></p>\n<p>On next level, you have to install <code class=\"language-text\">Firebase-CLI</code> with <code class=\"language-text\">npm</code>.</p>\n<pre class=\"language-js\"><code class=\"language-js\">npm install <span class=\"token operator\">-</span>g firebase<span class=\"token operator\">-</span>tools</code></pre>\n<p>After you install it, login with your credential as typing the following command</p>\n<pre class=\"language-js\"><code class=\"language-js\">firebase login</code></pre>\n<p>After successfully login, you have to <code class=\"language-text\">init</code> firebase into your project folder with </p>\n<pre class=\"language-js\"><code class=\"language-js\">firebase init</code></pre>\n<p>This command will ask some questions about the app. First of all is;</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Which Firebase CLI features do you want to set up for this folder?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Select <code class=\"language-text\">Hosting</code> option.</p>\n<p>Then you must associate the project with existing one or you have to create new one. As we already created one of them in <code class=\"language-text\">Firebase</code> named as <code class=\"language-text\">tutorial-blog-project</code> I will associate the project with this Firebase project. </p>\n<p>Select <code class=\"language-text\">use of existing project</code> then find <code class=\"language-text\">tutorial-blog-project</code> option. </p>\n<p>After selecting the project, there will be another series of the questions about Hosting Setup.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Your public directory is the folder (relative to your project directory) that\nwill contain Hosting assets to be uploaded with firebase deploy. If you\nhave a build process for your assets, use your build's output directory.</p>\n<p>What do you want to use as your public directory? (public)</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>This question is seeking a options that what folder of project you want to use it for production. As you know, in React, Vue or another web framework, we should use a <code class=\"language-text\">build</code> directory for production. In <code class=\"language-text\">Gridsome</code>, there's a directory called <code class=\"language-text\">dist</code> which contains the build. So I will use <code class=\"language-text\">dist</code> for this question.</p>\n<p>Another question is </p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Configure as a single-page app (rewrite all urls to /index.html)?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>You have to choose Y (Yes) for this question because it's a single-page-app basically and it routes all url's from the index.html.</p>\n<p>Then it will finish for the all process.</p>\n<p>Let's take a build for our newbie blog 🥳</p>\n<p>Type <code class=\"language-text\">gridsome build</code> then there will be created <code class=\"language-text\">dist</code> directory.</p>\n<p>Firebase is ready to serve it.</p>\n<p>Deploy it with <code class=\"language-text\">firebase deploy</code></p>\n<p>Yay 🤟 It's ready on <code class=\"language-text\">https://tutorial-blog-project.web.app/</code> or <code class=\"language-text\">https://whateveryourprojectname.web.app/</code> 😇</p>\n<p>It's done, so simple, so fast. </p>\n<p>I amazed when I learn the how <code class=\"language-text\">Firebase Hosting</code> is fast and simple to serve any website.</p>\n<p>For any change in blog, maybe you can add new blog post, you have to get a build with </p>\n<p><code class=\"language-text\">gridsome build</code> then you must deploy with <code class=\"language-text\">firebase deploy</code>. So simple 😎</p>\n<p>Now, let's look at another deployment to <code class=\"language-text\">Github Pages</code></p>\n<h2 id=\"hosting-into-github-pages\"><a href=\"#hosting-into-github-pages\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"icon icon-link\"></span></a>Hosting into Github Pages</h2>\n<p>There's a two simple way to hosting of <code class=\"language-text\">Gridsome</code> to Github Pages. Idealogy is so simple, you have to send your <code class=\"language-text\">build</code> directory into your Github repository named as your Github account. (It's important).</p>\n<p>There'a <code class=\"language-text\">npm</code> packages which handle this serves easily but I use manual way, I like this way much.</p>\n<p>Firsty you have to take a build for the project with <code class=\"language-text\">gridsome build</code></p>\n<p>There's an output named as <code class=\"language-text\">dist</code> directory.</p>\n<p>Let's assume that your github account name is <code class=\"language-text\">veryverysimplename</code>. I assume there is an repository named <code class=\"language-text\">veryverysimplename.github.io</code> in Github. So, assume that you create this named repository and clone it with </p>\n<p><code class=\"language-text\">git clone https://veryverysimplename/veryverysimplename.github.io</code> </p>\n<p>The directory is empty. Copy your <code class=\"language-text\">dist</code> directory into this <code class=\"language-text\">repository</code> folder.</p>\n<p>Then</p>\n<p> <code class=\"language-text\">git add .</code></p>\n<p> <code class=\"language-text\">git commit -m &quot;Add new blog page&quot;</code></p>\n<p> <code class=\"language-text\">git push -u origin master</code></p>\n<p> It's Done.</p>\n<p><img class=\"g-image g-image--lazy g-image--loading\" src=\"data:image/svg+xml,%3csvg fill='none' viewBox='0 0 1836 1148' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' xmlns:xlink='http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink'%3e%3cdefs%3e%3cfilter id='__svg-blur-3b1309e4f89fd3fe004cce91be9b6a09'%3e%3cfeGaussianBlur in='SourceGraphic' stdDeviation='40'/%3e%3c/filter%3e%3c/defs%3e%3cimage x='0' y='0' filter='url(%23__svg-blur-3b1309e4f89fd3fe004cce91be9b6a09)' width='1836' height='1148' xlink:href='data:image/png%3bbase6